{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1795115488095,"sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","part":{"partID":"prt_1f5389206001zdWLuu1lCxkDKV","sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","messageID":"msg_1f538876f001GZQzJkwHgjoT4u","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a5a021f00a66d8d4","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/455-expansive-depth-integration && ls REPO\\ RULES.md 2>/dev/null && sed -n '1,80p' REPO\\ RULES.md"},"output":"REPO RULES.md\n# REPO RULES\n\nRepo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\nbefore your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\nfile that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\nholds no rules of its own.\n\n---\n\n## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n\n1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n   specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n   edge case.\n5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n\n### Precedence\n\n| Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n|-------|--------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n| 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n| 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n| 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n\nA rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n`AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\nwhile what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\nis not.\n\n---\n\n## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n\n| You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n|-------------------|------|-----------|\n| Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n| Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n| Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n| Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n| Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n| Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n| Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n| Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n| End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n| Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n\n---\n\n## 3. INDEX\n\n| Rule | Summary |\n|------|---------|\n| [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n| [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n| [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n| [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n| [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n| [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n| [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n| [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n| [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n| [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n\nEach file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\npermit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n\n---\n\n## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n\n**In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\nthe posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\nreads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\nback to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\nsecond, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\ntrigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"REPO RULES.md\n# REPO RULES\n\nRepo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\nbefore your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\nfile that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\nholds no rules of its own.\n\n---\n\n## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n\n1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n   specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n   edge case.\n5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n\n### Precedence\n\n| Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n|-------|--------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n| 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n| 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n| 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n\nA rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n`AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\nwhile what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\nis not.\n\n---\n\n## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n\n| You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n|-------------------|------|-----------|\n| Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n| Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n| Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n| Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n| Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n| Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n| Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n| Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n| End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n| Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n\n---\n\n## 3. INDEX\n\n| Rule | Summary |\n|------|---------|\n| [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n| [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n| [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n| [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n| [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n| [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n| [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n| [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n| [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n| [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n\nEach file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\npermit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n\n---\n\n## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n\n**In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\nthe posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\nreads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\nback to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\nsecond, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\ntrigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/455-expansive-depth-integration && ls REPO\\\\ RULES.md 2>/dev/null && sed -n '1,80p' REPO\\\\ RULES.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1795115487750,"end":1795115488095}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1795115488962,"sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","part":{"id":"prt_1f53896c2001jsh5lygLvnKMTS","sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","messageID":"msg_1f538876f001GZQzJkwHgjoT4u","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"ba0796c77bd324ac74433dc40a053dc15c45ac8f","cost":0.000842106,"tokens":{"input":455,"output":87,"reasoning":237,"cache":{"read":193152,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1795115490528,"sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","part":{"id":"prt_1f5389cdf001EixzFwgs1EsxUa","sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","messageID":"msg_1f53896c7001QV2vhbhg9ODVuh","type":"step-start","snapshot":"ba0796c77bd324ac74433dc40a053dc15c45ac8f"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1795115491771,"sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","part":{"partID":"prt_1f538a149001HNiknZThpv6wQq","sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","messageID":"msg_1f53896c7001QV2vhbhg9ODVuh","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e2d4613ddd9dde1","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md, lines 1-175\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Prevent overengineering\"\n3: description: \"Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"restraint ladder\"\n6:   - \"reversal cost order\"\n7:   - \"does this need to exist\"\n8:   - \"build nothing\"\n9:   - \"future proof\"\n10:   - \"might need it later\"\n11:   - \"flexible\"\n12:   - \"best practice\"\n13:   - \"while we're here\"\n14:   - \"premature abstraction\"\n15:   - \"DRY it up\"\n16:   - \"two is not a pattern\"\n17:   - \"speculative optimization\"\n18:   - \"add a config option\"\n19:   - \"add a dependency\"\n20:   - \"feature flag\"\n21:   - \"new abstraction layer\"\n22:   - \"pre-write pass\"\n23:   - \"climbing sentence\"\n24:   - \"fallback for a constraint that does not exist\"\n25:   - \"over-engineering\"\n26: importance_tier: important\n27: contextType: reference\n28: version: 1.0.1.4\n29: ---\n30: \n31: # Rule: Prevent overengineering\n32: \n33: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the first write of anything new.\n34: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n35: \n36: ## Fires when\n37: \n38: - Adding a file, module, class, interface, abstraction, config option, feature flag, layer, or dependency.\n39: - Writing a spec or plan whose scope adds a file, module, option, layer, or dependency, before that scope is frozen.\n40: - Generalizing something that already works for its one caller.\n41: - Writing or thinking: *flexible*, *future-proof*, *scalable*, *extensible*, *might need*, *best practice*, *while we're here*.\n42: - Adding a test beyond the coverage floor, or wrapping something already callable.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem. Build the bigger thing only\n47: after naming what fails at the smaller one.**\n48: \n49: \"It's cleaner\" is not a failure. \"A future caller might\" is not a failure. A failure\n50: is something that breaks today, for a requirement that exists today.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 1. THE REVERSAL-COST ORDER\n55: \n56: Start at the cheapest move every time. **Take a costlier one only by writing the sentence\n57: that says what fails at the cheaper one** in the response, not just in your head.\n58: \n59: | Move | Cost when wrong |\n60: |------|-----------------|\n61: | **Build nothing:** existing behavior already meets the requirement | zero |\n62: | Change a value, constant, or config that exists | one line to revert |\n63: | Extend an existing function or module in place | contained to one unit |\n64: | Add a new function beside the existing ones | one new symbol to learn |\n65: | Add a new file or module | a new place readers must look |\n66: | Add an abstraction, interface, or indirection layer | every future reader pays the hop |\n67: | Add a dependency | permanent supply-chain, version and trust surface |\n68: \n69: > **This orders moves by what being wrong costs, and it is deliberately not the numbered\n70: > rung ladder.** For code, the code skill's universal quality standards are the\n71: > authoritative rungs. Route through `sk-code` to reach them. That ladder orders\n72: > *solution sources*: standard library, then native platform, then an installed\n73: > dependency. Two orderings, two axes, one authority: cite rung numbers from that file,\n74: > and cite moves by name from this one. A \"rung 2\" named here would mean something\n75: > different there.\n76: \n77: Building nothing is not a formality: a surprising share of requests are already satisfied\n78: by code that exists, and reading first is what reveals it. The sentence, written out:\n79: \n80: > \"Extending `parseConfig` in place fails, because the CLI and the daemon call it with\n81: > incompatible defaults today, so the change breaks the daemon. Adding a new function\n82: > beside it.\"\n83: \n84: If you cannot write that with a real symbol and a real caller in it, you are reaching\n85: past the move you can justify.\n86: \n87: **A short diff is not a cheaper move when the next reader has to decode it.** A one-liner that packs two decisions into one expression moves the cost to every later reader, who must rebuild the intent before they can check it. When two moves cost the same, the one that handles the edge cases correctly wins.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 2. THE PRE-WRITE PASS\n92: \n93: After reading the existing code, before the first edit, and before a spec or plan freezes its scope:\n94: \n95: 1. **Does this need to exist?** Walk §1 in order. Answer by naming the move and writing\n96:    its climbing sentence.\n97: 2. **What does it touch?** If the change can break a caller or a shared contract, name\n98:    the owning module, one real caller (`file:line`) and the contract that must not\n99:    break. Then list the tests, fixtures, config and exports the change must reach. No\n100:    real caller means the change is smaller than you think, or the code should not exist\n101:    either.\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 3. TWO SIGNALS `AGENTS.md` DOES NOT CARRY\n106: \n107: Its Restraint Signals table binds and is not repeated here.\n108: \n109: | Signal | What it means | Do this |\n110: |--------|---------------|---------|\n111: | A config option \"so we can change it later\" | a decision deferred into a permanent branch | Hardcode it (§4) |\n112: | A wrapper that only forwards arguments | indirection with no behavior | Call the thing directly |\n113: \n114: ---\n115: \n116: ## 4. SPECIFIC RESTRAINTS\n117: \n118: **Options.** Every option is a permanent branch in the code, a row in the docs, and an\n119: axis in the test matrix. Default to hardcoding. An option earns existence when **two\n120: real callers need different values today**.\n121: \n122: **Abstraction.** One instance is a case, two a coincidence, three a pattern. Abstracting\n123: at two buys a wrong abstraction more often than it saves a duplication.\n124: \n125: **Error handling.** Catch only what you can handle. A `try` that logs and re-raises, or\n126: swallows an exception into a default, converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.\n127: \n128: **Defensive checks.** Do not validate what the type system, the caller contract, or the\n129: layer above already guarantees. A null check on something never null tells the next\n130: reader it *is* sometimes null.\n131: \n132: **Tests.** The coverage floor and the earns-its-place bar are `AGENTS.md` §3. The ladder\n133: applies to test code exactly as to the code under test.\n134: \n135: **Performance.** No speculative optimization. Measure under stated conditions and report\n136: baseline and delta, or leave it alone.\n137: \n138: **Fallbacks.** A second code path for a constraint the target environment does not\n139: actually have is an untested branch that will rot. Add the no-install path, the offline\n140: path, or the degraded path only when you can name the environment that needs it. \"In\n141: case\" is not an environment.\n142: \n143: **Dependencies.** Prefer what the project has. A new one also takes the\n144: `blast-radius.md` pass, because installing mutates the environment.\n145: \n146: **Moves and merges.** Moved or merged code keeps its error handling and validation. Dropping a check during a move is a behavior change that needs its own reason.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 5. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n151: \n152: Restraint constrains *how much you build*, never *how much you deliver*.\n153: \n154: - **Not a license to under-deliver.** \"This part is unnecessary\" never authorizes\n155:   cutting it. Build the frozen scope **and** raise the amendment in the same response.\n156: - **Not a reason to skip real error handling.** A failure mode that happens is not\n157:   speculative.\n158: \n159: - **Not a reason to cut accessibility.** Restraint never cuts accessibility on user-facing UI: interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 6. SELF-CHECK\n164: \n165: - [ ] Named the move and wrote the climbing sentence for every move past \"build nothing\".\n166: - [ ] When two moves cost the same, I took the one that handles the edge cases correctly. A shorter diff that the next reader must decode did not count as cheaper.\n167: - [ ] Every new option, abstraction and dependency has a caller that needs it **today**.\n168: - [ ] Nothing is justified only by a future nobody asked for.\n169: - [ ] If I judged part of the scope unnecessary, I built it anyway and said so.\n170: - [ ] Any fallback path I added names the environment that requires it.\n171: - [ ] Where the change touches a caller or a shared contract, I named the owner, one real caller and the contract before editing. I listed the tests, fixtures, config and exports it must reach.\n172: - [ ] Every catch handles what it catches, and no check re-validates what a type or a caller contract already guarantees.\n173: - [ ] Every performance claim carries a measurement, and every new dependency names what the project's own tools could not do.\n174: - [ ] Moved or merged code kept its error handling and validation. Every check dropped during a move has its own stated reason.\n175: - [ ] Restraint did not cut accessibility on user-facing UI, where interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md, lines 1-175\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Prevent overengineering\"\n3: description: \"Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"restraint ladder\"\n6:   - \"reversal cost order\"\n7:   - \"does this need to exist\"\n8:   - \"build nothing\"\n9:   - \"future proof\"\n10:   - \"might need it later\"\n11:   - \"flexible\"\n12:   - \"best practice\"\n13:   - \"while we're here\"\n14:   - \"premature abstraction\"\n15:   - \"DRY it up\"\n16:   - \"two is not a pattern\"\n17:   - \"speculative optimization\"\n18:   - \"add a config option\"\n19:   - \"add a dependency\"\n20:   - \"feature flag\"\n21:   - \"new abstraction layer\"\n22:   - \"pre-write pass\"\n23:   - \"climbing sentence\"\n24:   - \"fallback for a constraint that does not exist\"\n25:   - \"over-engineering\"\n26: importance_tier: important\n27: contextType: reference\n28: version: 1.0.1.4\n29: ---\n30: \n31: # Rule: Prevent overengineering\n32: \n33: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the first write of anything new.\n34: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n35: \n36: ## Fires when\n37: \n38: - Adding a file, module, class, interface, abstraction, config option, feature flag, layer, or dependency.\n39: - Writing a spec or plan whose scope adds a file, module, option, layer, or dependency, before that scope is frozen.\n40: - Generalizing something that already works for its one caller.\n41: - Writing or thinking: *flexible*, *future-proof*, *scalable*, *extensible*, *might need*, *best practice*, *while we're here*.\n42: - Adding a test beyond the coverage floor, or wrapping something already callable.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem. Build the bigger thing only\n47: after naming what fails at the smaller one.**\n48: \n49: \"It's cleaner\" is not a failure. \"A future caller might\" is not a failure. A failure\n50: is something that breaks today, for a requirement that exists today.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 1. THE REVERSAL-COST ORDER\n55: \n56: Start at the cheapest move every time. **Take a costlier one only by writing the sentence\n57: that says what fails at the cheaper one** in the response, not just in your head.\n58: \n59: | Move | Cost when wrong |\n60: |------|-----------------|\n61: | **Build nothing:** existing behavior already meets the requirement | zero |\n62: | Change a value, constant, or config that exists | one line to revert |\n63: | Extend an existing function or module in place | contained to one unit |\n64: | Add a new function beside the existing ones | one new symbol to learn |\n65: | Add a new file or module | a new place readers must look |\n66: | Add an abstraction, interface, or indirection layer | every future reader pays the hop |\n67: | Add a dependency | permanent supply-chain, version and trust surface |\n68: \n69: > **This orders moves by what being wrong costs, and it is deliberately not the numbered\n70: > rung ladder.** For code, the code skill's universal quality standards are the\n71: > authoritative rungs. Route through `sk-code` to reach them. That ladder orders\n72: > *solution sources*: standard library, then native platform, then an installed\n73: > dependency. Two orderings, two axes, one authority: cite rung numbers from that file,\n74: > and cite moves by name from this one. A \"rung 2\" named here would mean something\n75: > different there.\n76: \n77: Building nothing is not a formality: a surprising share of requests are already satisfied\n78: by code that exists, and reading first is what reveals it. The sentence, written out:\n79: \n80: > \"Extending `parseConfig` in place fails, because the CLI and the daemon call it with\n81: > incompatible defaults today, so the change breaks the daemon. Adding a new function\n82: > beside it.\"\n83: \n84: If you cannot write that with a real symbol and a real caller in it, you are reaching\n85: past the move you can justify.\n86: \n87: **A short diff is not a cheaper move when the next reader has to decode it.** A one-liner that packs two decisions into one expression moves the cost to every later reader, who must rebuild the intent before they can check it. When two moves cost the same, the one that handles the edge cases correctly wins.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 2. THE PRE-WRITE PASS\n92: \n93: After reading the existing code, before the first edit, and before a spec or plan freezes its scope:\n94: \n95: 1. **Does this need to exist?** Walk §1 in order. Answer by naming the move and writing\n96:    its climbing sentence.\n97: 2. **What does it touch?** If the change can break a caller or a shared contract, name\n98:    the owning module, one real caller (`file:line`) and the contract that must not\n99:    break. Then list the tests, fixtures, config and exports the change must reach. No\n100:    real caller means the change is smaller than you think, or the code should not exist\n101:    either.\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 3. TWO SIGNALS `AGENTS.md` DOES NOT CARRY\n106: \n107: Its Restraint Signals table binds and is not repeated here.\n108: \n109: | Signal | What it means | Do this |\n110: |--------|---------------|---------|\n111: | A config option \"so we can change it later\" | a decision deferred into a permanent branch | Hardcode it (§4) |\n112: | A wrapper that only forwards arguments | indirection with no behavior | Call the thing directly |\n113: \n114: ---\n115: \n116: ## 4. SPECIFIC RESTRAINTS\n117: \n118: **Options.** Every option is a permanent branch in the code, a row in the docs, and an\n119: axis in the test matrix. Default to hardcoding. An option earns existence when **two\n120: real callers need different values today**.\n121: \n122: **Abstraction.** One instance is a case, two a coincidence, three a pattern. Abstracting\n123: at two buys a wrong abstraction more often than it saves a duplication.\n124: \n125: **Error handling.** Catch only what you can handle. A `try` that logs and re-raises, or\n126: swallows an exception into a default, converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.\n127: \n128: **Defensive checks.** Do not validate what the type system, the caller contract, or the\n129: layer above already guarantees. A null check on something never null tells the next\n130: reader it *is* sometimes null.\n131: \n132: **Tests.** The coverage floor and the earns-its-place bar are `AGENTS.md` §3. The ladder\n133: applies to test code exactly as to the code under test.\n134: \n135: **Performance.** No speculative optimization. Measure under stated conditions and report\n136: baseline and delta, or leave it alone.\n137: \n138: **Fallbacks.** A second code path for a constraint the target environment does not\n139: actually have is an untested branch that will rot. Add the no-install path, the offline\n140: path, or the degraded path only when you can name the environment that needs it. \"In\n141: case\" is not an environment.\n142: \n143: **Dependencies.** Prefer what the project has. A new one also takes the\n144: `blast-radius.md` pass, because installing mutates the environment.\n145: \n146: **Moves and merges.** Moved or merged code keeps its error handling and validation. Dropping a check during a move is a behavior change that needs its own reason.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 5. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n151: \n152: Restraint constrains *how much you build*, never *how much you deliver*.\n153: \n154: - **Not a license to under-deliver.** \"This part is unnecessary\" never authorizes\n155:   cutting it. Build the frozen scope **and** raise the amendment in the same response.\n156: - **Not a reason to skip real error handling.** A failure mode that happens is not\n157:   speculative.\n158: \n159: - **Not a reason to cut accessibility.** Restraint never cuts accessibility on user-facing UI: interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 6. SELF-CHECK\n164: \n165: - [ ] Named the move and wrote the climbing sentence for every move past \"build nothing\".\n166: - [ ] When two moves cost the same, I took the one that handles the edge cases correctly. A shorter diff that the next reader must decode did not count as cheaper.\n167: - [ ] Every new option, abstraction and dependency has a caller that needs it **today**.\n168: - [ ] Nothing is justified only by a future nobody asked for.\n169: - [ ] If I judged part of the scope unnecessary, I built it anyway and said so.\n170: - [ ] Any fallback path I added names the environment that requires it.\n171: - [ ] Where the change touches a caller or a shared contract, I named the owner, one real caller and the contract before editing. I listed the tests, fixtures, config and exports it must reach.\n172: - [ ] Every catch handles what it catches, and no check re-validates what a type or a caller contract already guarantees.\n173: - [ ] Every performance claim carries a measurement, and every new dependency names what the project's own tools could not do.\n174: - [ ] Moved or merged code kept its error handling and validation. Every check dropped during a move has its own stated reason.\n175: - [ ] Restraint did not cut accessibility on user-facing UI, where interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \".skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1795115491657,"end":1795115491771}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1795115491778,"sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","part":{"partID":"prt_1f538a0ea001V2dskP0FlfN8ED","sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","messageID":"msg_1f53896c7001QV2vhbhg9ODVuh","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-86a9bfe579b224b2","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md, lines 1-236\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\n3: description: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"observed derived inferred\"\n6:   - \"how a green run lies\"\n7:   - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n8:   - \"negative control\"\n9:   - \"watch it fail first\"\n10:   - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n11:   - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n12:   - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n13:   - \"the tool reported success\"\n14:   - \"claiming done\"\n15:   - \"final-state proof\"\n16:   - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n17:   - \"reason from data not memory\"\n18:   - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n19:   - \"honest close-out\"\n20:   - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n21:   - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n22:   - \"unconfirmed cause\"\n23:   - \"suspected cause\"\n24:   - \"cause then fix\"\n25:   - \"tests pass\"\n26:   - \"verify before claiming done\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.1.1.6\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Evidence and proof\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n40: - About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n41: - A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n42: - About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n43: - You are closing out a turn.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\n48: carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 1. THREE TIERS\n53: \n54: | Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n55: |------|---------|--------------------|\n56: | **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n57: | **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n58: | **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n59: \n60: Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\n61: What to do when a claim cannot be resolved is\n62: [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n63: \n64: **A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would\n65: have left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim. It\n66: belongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.\n67: A search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim\n68: is absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read\n69: the span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:\n70: derive it here, at the total's own level. A match that never distinguished roles: compare\n71: what the two sides are, not the digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the\n72: honest output is INFERRED plus the step that would confirm it.\n73: \n74: When a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix\n75: order holds. Where no run confirms the cause, it is INFERRED: label it as suspected and\n76: name the next check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding with its receipt. There\n77: is no unmarked case.\n78: \n79: The failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next\n80: reader treats as settled and stops testing.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n85: \n86: **A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n87: \n88: - Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n89: - **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n90:   start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n91:   and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n92: - **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n93:   the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n94: - Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n95:   authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n100: \n101: 1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n102:    before the first test body.\n103: 2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n104:    that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n105:    is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n106:    source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all. Re-run with `-a`\n107:    before concluding.\n108: 3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n109:    metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n110: 4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n111:    snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n116: \n117: **Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\n118: to prove the fix.** A check that passed before your change and passes after it proves\n119: nothing about your change. If reproducing is unsafe, say so and name what you used\n120: instead.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 5. BASELINES\n125: \n126: \"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n127: \n128: 1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n129:    changing anything.\n130: 2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n131: 3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n132: \n133: Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\n134: delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n139: \n140: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n141:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n142:   you missed.\n143: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n144:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n145: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n146:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n151: \n152: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n153: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n154: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n155: can explain. Confirming the symptom is\n156: [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n157: [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n162: \n163: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n164: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n165: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n166: \n167: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n168: the standard.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n173: \n174: Before any completion claim:\n175: \n176: - [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n177: - [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n178: - [ ] Every outcome the request and its amendments ask for maps to a criterion that passed, or the close-out names it as not done. Reread the request to find them, because the criteria list can miss one.\n179: - [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n180:       output, no unrelated file.\n181: - [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n182:       blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n187: \n188: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. What it holds is listed once, in\n189: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n190: \n191: And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\n192: was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\n193: done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging. **The first report is the honest\n194: one:** a claim that becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was\n195: written, not when it was corrected. What is now the operator's to do is a separate\n196: report, see [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n197: \n198: ---\n199: \n200: ## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n201: \n202: Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n203: \n204: **Reasoning from what the code probably does.** Open it. The cost of being wrong here\n205: is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\n206: that looks like a different bug.\n207: \n208: **Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\n209: a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\n210: code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n211: *what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\n212: defect. A turn recalled through the history lookup is a claim too: it shows what was\n213: true when it ran, not what is true now.\n214: \n215: Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\n216: does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\n217: after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope.\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n222: \n223: - [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n224: - [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n225: - [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n226: - [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n227: - [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n228: - [ ] The close-out names any known residual risk the operator must weigh. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n229: - [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n230: - [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n231: - [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.\n232: - [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.\n233: - [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.\n234: - [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.\n235: - [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.\n236: - [ ] I reread the request, and every outcome it asks for is met or named as not done.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md, lines 1-236\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\n3: description: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"observed derived inferred\"\n6:   - \"how a green run lies\"\n7:   - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n8:   - \"negative control\"\n9:   - \"watch it fail first\"\n10:   - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n11:   - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n12:   - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n13:   - \"the tool reported success\"\n14:   - \"claiming done\"\n15:   - \"final-state proof\"\n16:   - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n17:   - \"reason from data not memory\"\n18:   - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n19:   - \"honest close-out\"\n20:   - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n21:   - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n22:   - \"unconfirmed cause\"\n23:   - \"suspected cause\"\n24:   - \"cause then fix\"\n25:   - \"tests pass\"\n26:   - \"verify before claiming done\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.1.1.6\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Evidence and proof\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n40: - About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n41: - A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n42: - About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n43: - You are closing out a turn.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\n48: carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 1. THREE TIERS\n53: \n54: | Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n55: |------|---------|--------------------|\n56: | **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n57: | **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n58: | **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n59: \n60: Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\n61: What to do when a claim cannot be resolved is\n62: [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n63: \n64: **A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would\n65: have left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim. It\n66: belongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.\n67: A search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim\n68: is absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read\n69: the span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:\n70: derive it here, at the total's own level. A match that never distinguished roles: compare\n71: what the two sides are, not the digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the\n72: honest output is INFERRED plus the step that would confirm it.\n73: \n74: When a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix\n75: order holds. Where no run confirms the cause, it is INFERRED: label it as suspected and\n76: name the next check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding with its receipt. There\n77: is no unmarked case.\n78: \n79: The failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next\n80: reader treats as settled and stops testing.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n85: \n86: **A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n87: \n88: - Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n89: - **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n90:   start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n91:   and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n92: - **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n93:   the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n94: - Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n95:   authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n100: \n101: 1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n102:    before the first test body.\n103: 2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n104:    that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n105:    is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n106:    source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all. Re-run with `-a`\n107:    before concluding.\n108: 3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n109:    metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n110: 4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n111:    snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n116: \n117: **Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\n118: to prove the fix.** A check that passed before your change and passes after it proves\n119: nothing about your change. If reproducing is unsafe, say so and name what you used\n120: instead.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 5. BASELINES\n125: \n126: \"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n127: \n128: 1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n129:    changing anything.\n130: 2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n131: 3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n132: \n133: Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\n134: delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n139: \n140: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n141:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n142:   you missed.\n143: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n144:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n145: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n146:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n151: \n152: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n153: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n154: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n155: can explain. Confirming the symptom is\n156: [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n157: [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n162: \n163: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n164: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n165: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n166: \n167: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n168: the standard.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n173: \n174: Before any completion claim:\n175: \n176: - [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n177: - [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n178: - [ ] Every outcome the request and its amendments ask for maps to a criterion that passed, or the close-out names it as not done. Reread the request to find them, because the criteria list can miss one.\n179: - [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n180:       output, no unrelated file.\n181: - [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n182:       blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n187: \n188: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. What it holds is listed once, in\n189: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n190: \n191: And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\n192: was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\n193: done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging. **The first report is the honest\n194: one:** a claim that becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was\n195: written, not when it was corrected. What is now the operator's to do is a separate\n196: report, see [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n197: \n198: ---\n199: \n200: ## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n201: \n202: Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n203: \n204: **Reasoning from what the code probably does.** Open it. The cost of being wrong here\n205: is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\n206: that looks like a different bug.\n207: \n208: **Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\n209: a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\n210: code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n211: *what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\n212: defect. A turn recalled through the history lookup is a claim too: it shows what was\n213: true when it ran, not what is true now.\n214: \n215: Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\n216: does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\n217: after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope.\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n222: \n223: - [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n224: - [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n225: - [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n226: - [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n227: - [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n228: - [ ] The close-out names any known residual risk the operator must weigh. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n229: - [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n230: - [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n231: - [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.\n232: - [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.\n233: - [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.\n234: - [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.\n235: - [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.\n236: - [ ] I reread the request, and every outcome it asks for is met or named as not done."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \".skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1795115491562,"end":1795115491778}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1795115492765,"sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","part":{"id":"prt_1f538a59d001bNAaIvS7rFdBT4","sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","messageID":"msg_1f53896c7001QV2vhbhg9ODVuh","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"ba0796c77bd324ac74433dc40a053dc15c45ac8f","cost":0.001137408,"tokens":{"input":2604,"output":87,"reasoning":190,"cache":{"read":193536,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1795115494542,"sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","part":{"id":"prt_1f538ac8e0017WgAnIRCa9yuP2","sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","messageID":"msg_1f538a5a2001FP8YD4Im8WK3Ex","type":"step-start","snapshot":"ba0796c77bd324ac74433dc40a053dc15c45ac8f"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1795115500834,"sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","part":{"partID":"prt_1f538c120001Q7N6V3LzuboDva","sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","messageID":"msg_1f538a5a2001FP8YD4Im8WK3Ex","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-963a51b8fb12bfb9","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/455-expansive-depth-integration && grep -rn \"thought\\|status\" specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/008-review-dimension-coverage/spec.md | head -10; echo \"===011 spec dimension universe===\"; grep -rn \"universe\\|dimension\" specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md | grep -i \"answered\\|cover\" | head -10"},"output":"===011 spec dimension universe===\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:3:description: \"Deep-review has no question surface, so the research frontier cannot be reused as is: a review run cannot widen beyond its four fixed dimensions or decompose a large target. This phase adds the review side of --depth-mode=expansive, admitting leaf-proposed sub-targets into candidateCoverage and searchDebt, with a review proposal channel, a review Next Focus branch, the stop veto and pivot seed changes, and default runs byte-identical.\"\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:23:A deep-review run covers four fixed dimensions over one configured target and has no question set, inbox or other surface that can grow mid-run, so a target like \"the last 100 commits\" cannot be split into subsystems and batches. This phase builds Slice K of the parent research: the review side of `--depth-mode=expansive`, which seeds depth-1 sub-target nodes before the first iteration, admits typed `frontier_proposal` rows from a new review pack channel into `candidateCoverage` and `searchDebt` plus a review `registry.frontier`, keeps node status in its own append-only store, steers the dispatched dimension and focus through `step_read_state`, extends the `frontierOpen` veto to review stop candidates and changes the `divergent-review-pivot.ts` seeds. Fan-out runs get the seed before spawn, a `--depth-mode` passthrough, a recorded partition and a merged review `frontier`. The proof is a golden test that keeps default review runs byte-identical, a review admission suite, `review-depth-convergence` cases and a pilot on the last-100-commits target with fan-out partitioning of its seven subsystem nodes.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:25:**Key Decisions**: admit into the review coverage surfaces, never an inbox; reuse the research phase's flag, admission helper, node-store row shape, seed step, depth budget and veto rather than a second design; steer through `next_dimension` and a `{frontier_block}` pack variable, because review dispatch reads `next_dimension` (`deep-review.yaml:904`) and the reducer's Next Focus is advisory text.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:27:**Critical Dependencies**: phases `003-review-min-iterations-floor`, `004-focus-hijack-fix`, `008-review-dimension-coverage`, `009-fanout-review-coverage-merge`, `010-research-frontier-mode` and `012-pivot-hygiene` must land first. 003 adds the review floor the veto sits after, 004 rewrites the review focus block this phase extends, and 012 rewrites the pivot branch and `divergent-review-pivot.ts` this phase gates.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:42:| **Depends On** | `003-review-min-iterations-floor`, `004-focus-hijack-fix`, `008-review-dimension-coverage`, `009-fanout-review-coverage-merge`, `010-research-frontier-mode`, `012-pivot-hygiene` |\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:53:The review coverage universe is `reviewDimensions`, normalized once at init and locked, and the review reducer has no `inbox`, `keyQuestions` or `openQuestions` surface, so nothing a leaf discovers can become counted review work. The review pivot's seed vocabulary is closed and its seat mandates forbid expanding the configured target, so a large target is reviewed as one undivided unit on four axes. A research-style inbox admission would be read by nothing on review.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:83:- Add the review reducer frontier projection read from the node store, the close rule, per-node dimension coverage and the Next Focus branch, and extend the `frontierOpen` veto to review stop candidates.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:84:- Steer dispatch: under the flag, `step_read_state` extracts the active frontier node from `registry.frontier` and sets `next_dimension` to that node's next uncovered dimension, and the review pack carries the node through a `{frontier_block}` variable.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:92:- Carrying the existing review coverage surfaces through the fan-out merge and deciding custom review dimensions - owned by `009-fanout-review-coverage-merge` and `008-review-dimension-coverage`. The merged review `frontier` is new data and is built here.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:105:| `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review.yaml` | Modify | `depth_mode` in `user_inputs` (`:54-58`) and `step_preflight_contract` (`:356`); keys in `step_create_config` (`:674`); review `step_frontier_seed` before `step_fanout_spawn` (`:304`) and in `phase_init`; `--depth-mode {depth_mode}` on the `fanout-run.cjs` command (`:327-341`); in `step_read_state` (`:889`) a `depth_mode` extract, an `active_frontier_node` extract from `registry.frontier`, and a flag-gated case in the `next_dimension` rule (`:904`) that returns the node's next uncovered dimension; `--depth-mode` on the `convergence.cjs` call (`:989`); `frontier_block` in `render_prompt_pack.variables` (`:1450`), modeled on `review_focus_block`; review admission step after verify-iteration and before the reduce step; expansive STUCK_RECOVERY sub-branch keeping `deep_review.recovery_started`; pivot gating in the divergent branch (`:1150`) as 012 left it |\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"===011 spec dimension universe===\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:3:description: \"Deep-review has no question surface, so the research frontier cannot be reused as is: a review run cannot widen beyond its four fixed dimensions or decompose a large target. This phase adds the review side of --depth-mode=expansive, admitting leaf-proposed sub-targets into candidateCoverage and searchDebt, with a review proposal channel, a review Next Focus branch, the stop veto and pivot seed changes, and default runs byte-identical.\"\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:23:A deep-review run covers four fixed dimensions over one configured target and has no question set, inbox or other surface that can grow mid-run, so a target like \"the last 100 commits\" cannot be split into subsystems and batches. This phase builds Slice K of the parent research: the review side of `--depth-mode=expansive`, which seeds depth-1 sub-target nodes before the first iteration, admits typed `frontier_proposal` rows from a new review pack channel into `candidateCoverage` and `searchDebt` plus a review `registry.frontier`, keeps node status in its own append-only store, steers the dispatched dimension and focus through `step_read_state`, extends the `frontierOpen` veto to review stop candidates and changes the `divergent-review-pivot.ts` seeds. Fan-out runs get the seed before spawn, a `--depth-mode` passthrough, a recorded partition and a merged review `frontier`. The proof is a golden test that keeps default review runs byte-identical, a review admission suite, `review-depth-convergence` cases and a pilot on the last-100-commits target with fan-out partitioning of its seven subsystem nodes.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:25:**Key Decisions**: admit into the review coverage surfaces, never an inbox; reuse the research phase's flag, admission helper, node-store row shape, seed step, depth budget and veto rather than a second design; steer through `next_dimension` and a `{frontier_block}` pack variable, because review dispatch reads `next_dimension` (`deep-review.yaml:904`) and the reducer's Next Focus is advisory text.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:27:**Critical Dependencies**: phases `003-review-min-iterations-floor`, `004-focus-hijack-fix`, `008-review-dimension-coverage`, `009-fanout-review-coverage-merge`, `010-research-frontier-mode` and `012-pivot-hygiene` must land first. 003 adds the review floor the veto sits after, 004 rewrites the review focus block this phase extends, and 012 rewrites the pivot branch and `divergent-review-pivot.ts` this phase gates.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:42:| **Depends On** | `003-review-min-iterations-floor`, `004-focus-hijack-fix`, `008-review-dimension-coverage`, `009-fanout-review-coverage-merge`, `010-research-frontier-mode`, `012-pivot-hygiene` |\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:53:The review coverage universe is `reviewDimensions`, normalized once at init and locked, and the review reducer has no `inbox`, `keyQuestions` or `openQuestions` surface, so nothing a leaf discovers can become counted review work. The review pivot's seed vocabulary is closed and its seat mandates forbid expanding the configured target, so a large target is reviewed as one undivided unit on four axes. A research-style inbox admission would be read by nothing on review.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:83:- Add the review reducer frontier projection read from the node store, the close rule, per-node dimension coverage and the Next Focus branch, and extend the `frontierOpen` veto to review stop candidates.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:84:- Steer dispatch: under the flag, `step_read_state` extracts the active frontier node from `registry.frontier` and sets `next_dimension` to that node's next uncovered dimension, and the review pack carries the node through a `{frontier_block}` variable.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:92:- Carrying the existing review coverage surfaces through the fan-out merge and deciding custom review dimensions - owned by `009-fanout-review-coverage-merge` and `008-review-dimension-coverage`. The merged review `frontier` is new data and is built here.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md:105:| `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review.yaml` | Modify | `depth_mode` in `user_inputs` (`:54-58`) and `step_preflight_contract` (`:356`); keys in `step_create_config` (`:674`); review `step_frontier_seed` before `step_fanout_spawn` (`:304`) and in `phase_init`; `--depth-mode {depth_mode}` on the `fanout-run.cjs` command (`:327-341`); in `step_read_state` (`:889`) a `depth_mode` extract, an `active_frontier_node` extract from `registry.frontier`, and a flag-gated case in the `next_dimension` rule (`:904`) that returns the node's next uncovered dimension; `--depth-mode` on the `convergence.cjs` call (`:989`); `frontier_block` in `render_prompt_pack.variables` (`:1450`), modeled on `review_focus_block`; review admission step after verify-iteration and before the reduce step; expansive STUCK_RECOVERY sub-branch keeping `deep_review.recovery_started`; pivot gating in the divergent branch (`:1150`) as 012 left it |\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/455-expansive-depth-integration && grep -rn \\\"thought\\\\|status\\\" specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/008-review-dimension-coverage/spec.md | head -10; echo \\\"===011 spec dimension universe===\\\"; grep -rn \\\"universe\\\\|dimension\\\" specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/011-review-frontier-mode/spec.md | grep -i \\\"answered\\\\|cover\\\" | head -10\"}"},"time":{"start":1795115499808,"end":1795115500834}}}}
