{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1795115944329,"sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","part":{"id":"prt_1f53f8989001ApbPQp7UxFxAsH","sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","messageID":"msg_1f53f7f7c001bA1r9SJh71EUZ6","type":"step-start","snapshot":"1b55ebf96ec914463b511baa66040722b953aaad"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1795115956208,"sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","part":{"partID":"prt_1f53f8a6e001aF5qIyuiFzzw5W","sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","messageID":"msg_1f53f7f7c001bA1r9SJh71EUZ6","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-98c3d73031a8cf19","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 5 - F-1-005 security: reducer, gateway-facing state, upsert and dispatch trust boundaries\n\n## Dispatcher\n\n- Mode: review, detached fan-out lineage `deepseek` (session `fanout-deepseek-1795113418367-pvzn7z`, generation 1, lineageMode new).\n- Frontier node: F-1-005, dimension security. Node scope: `reduce-state.cjs` (review and research), `upsert.cjs`, `convergence.cjs`, `verify-iteration.cjs`, `dispatch-iteration.cjs` and the five `runtime/lib` ledger, projection and reducer folders.\n- Operator review focus covered in this iteration: overengineering (the two reducers' path/state helpers compared), operator UX (reducer warnings and refusal text for corrupt state), cross-CLI/OS parity (POSIX-only path classification in the new source-quality classifier, and the temp-dir fallback in dispatch).\n\n## Files Reviewed\n\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/upsert.cjs` -- state/delta readers, source-quality classifier, finding-id folding, orphan-edge skip, namespace writes.\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs` -- delta loader, frontier store reader, registry/dashboard/strategy writers, lineage and extension state.\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs` -- question merge, frontier store reader, path handling.\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/convergence.cjs` -- stop facts read from the registry, graph and state rows.\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/verify-iteration.cjs` -- route-proof sources and delta fallback.\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/dispatch-iteration.cjs` -- prompt-file reads, temp-dir creation, flag validation and env pass-through.\n- Traceability sources: `007-source-diversity-quality-class` REQ-001..REQ-005, `001-completed-run-extension` REQ-002, `006-state-log-leaf-fields` REQ-001..REQ-004.\n\n## Findings - New\n\n### P0 Findings\n\nNone.\n\n### P1 Findings\n\nNone.\n\n### P2 Findings\n\n1. **Source-quality classification assumes POSIX separators, so Windows-separated paths lose their spec and test classes** -- .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/upsert.cjs:330 -- `classifySourceLocation` strips a leading `./` and splits the path on `/` only, then tests `segments[0] === 'specs'` and `segments.some(segment => TEST_DIR_NAMES.has(segment))`. A path written with backslashes, as a Windows leaf would cite it (`specs\\\\foo\\\\spec.md` or `src\\\\tests\\\\fixture.ts`), becomes a single segment; `specs\\\\...` then falls through to `SKILL_DOC` because of `.md`, and a test-directory file falls through to `REPO_CODE`. The graph's source-diversity signal then reads a different class set for the same repository on Windows than on macOS or Linux, and the REQ-002 class table is only pinned for POSIX paths.\nFinding class: class-of-bug\nScope proof: the only separator handling in the function is `split('/')` (`:330`); `path.extname` still finds the extension because it accepts both separators, so the function returns a class rather than failing, and no test in the REQ-002 case uses a backslash path.\nAffected surface hints: [\"upsert.cjs classifySourceLocation\", \"coverage-graph-signals sourceDiversity\", \"007 REQ-002 class table\", \"Windows leaf citation form\"]\nCase: a research leaf on Windows cites `specs\\\\system-deep-loop\\\\...\\\\spec.md` and `src\\\\tests\\\\fixture.ts`; the upsert stores `skill-doc` and `repo-code` where a macOS run of the same files stores `repo-spec` and `repo-test`, so a question that cites both a spec and a test can score 2.0 on one host and 1.0 on another.\n\n## Traceability Checks\n\n| Protocol | Status | Evidence |\n|----------|--------|----------|\n| `spec_code` | partial | `007` REQ-001..REQ-005, `001` REQ-002 and `006` REQ-001..REQ-004 match the code on POSIX paths and on the extension ledger shape. The iteration 3 REQ-007 admission gap remains the only partial; this iteration adds the Windows path-shape gap above as an advisory. |\n| `checklist_evidence` | partial | `007` REQ-002's class table is met for the POSIX fixtures it names; AC-008 remains the outstanding partial. |\n\n## Integration Evidence\n\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/upsert.cjs:383-410` reads the state log and delta only when the review pipeline passes `--state-log`/`--delta-dir`, and rejects those flags for research; the namespace it writes is `{ specFolder, loopType, sessionId }`.\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2840-2860` loads every delta file under the packet's `deltas/` directory and hands the flattened rows to one registry build; a malformed delta can only affect derived output inside the packet.\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/verify-iteration.cjs:552-570` prefers the projected route proof and falls back to the delta only when the state row fails it, with the fallback named on stderr.\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/dispatch-iteration.cjs:273-300` reads the prompt file the workflow staged and never interpolates argv values into a shell; the executor command is built from an argv array.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- `dispatch-iteration.cjs:835` creates its review baseline directory under `process.env.TMPDIR || '/tmp'` rather than `os.tmpdir()`; the line predates this range, and on a host without `/tmp` it would fail rather than escape the packet, so it is recorded as a portability observation, not a finding.\n- The reducer writes registry, dashboard and strategy through `fs.writeFileSync` without an lstat symlink guard; a leaf-writable lineage directory could hold a symlink at one of those names. The write helpers predate the range, and the orchestrator's containment snapshot covers outside-lineage effects, so this is recorded as a residual hardening item rather than a range finding.\n- A corrupt lineage state log aborts the whole merge rather than degrading that lineage; the merge's own per-lineage reconstruction path documents why that is undesirable, but the throwing readers predate the range.\n\n## Confirmed-Clean Surfaces\n\n- The upsert never trusts a leaf-supplied `quality_class` for research SOURCE nodes; the derived class overwrites it, and the closed vocabulary is imported from the graph query module.\n- Finding evidence is derived only from a `file:line` ref, and an edge whose endpoint neither the batch nor the stored graph declares is skipped with a stderr notice instead of failing the batch.\n- The review and research reducers read all state under the packet's own artifact directory; no flag can redirect the registry or dashboard path.\n- `convergence.cjs` reads stop facts from the registry and state rows only; no new external input reaches the stop decision.\n- No range-introduced script in this node invokes a shell or a GNU-only tool; the new code uses Node `fs`/`path` and JSON parsing.\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- A symlink-following write through the reducer outputs as a range finding: the writers predate the range and the effect stays inside or is caught by containment.\n- A corrupt-lineage merge abort as a range finding: the readers predate the range and fail closed.\n- The `/tmp` fallback as a range finding: the line predates the range; recorded as a portability observation.\n- Treating leaf-authored finding paths as an injection boundary: they are already rendered as untrusted prose in every review artifact, and no new evaluator consumes them.\n\n## Next Focus\n\n- Dimension: security on F-1-007 (pivot and coverage-graph trust boundaries).\n- Reason: F-1-005 security is clear apart from the Windows advisory; F-1-007 is the last node without security coverage and its pivot state, paths and graph signals are the remaining untrusted-input surface.\n- Required evidence: `divergent-pivot.ts` persisted-config parsing and path containment, `divergent-pivot.cjs` argv parsing, `frontier-admit.cjs graph` output and `coverage-graph` metadata filtering.\n- Rotation status: correctness covered on all nodes; security covered on F-1-003 and F-1-005.\n\nReview verdict: CONDITIONAL\n","path":"specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/review/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/review/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/review/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Iteration 5 - F-1-005 security: reducer, gateway-facing state, upsert and dispatch trust boundaries\\n\\n## Dispatcher\\n\\n- Mode: review, detached fan-out lineage `deepseek` (session `fanout-deepseek-1795113418367-pvzn7z`, generation 1, lineageMode new).\\n- Frontier node: F-1-005, dimension security. Node scope: `reduce-state.cjs` (review and research), `upsert.cjs`, `convergence.cjs`, `verify-iteration.cjs`, `dispatch-iteration.cjs` and the five `runtime/lib` ledger, projection and reducer folders.\\n- Operator review focus covered in this iteration: overengineering (the two reducers' path/state helpers compared), operator UX (reducer warnings and refusal text for corrupt state), cross-CLI/OS parity (POSIX-only path classification in the new source-quality classifier, and the temp-dir fallback in dispatch).\\n\\n## Files Reviewed\\n\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/upsert.cjs` -- state/delta readers, source-quality classifier, finding-id folding, orphan-edge skip, namespace writes.\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs` -- delta loader, frontier store reader, registry/dashboard/strategy writers, lineage and extension state.\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs` -- question merge, frontier store reader, path handling.\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/convergence.cjs` -- stop facts read from the registry, graph and state rows.\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/verify-iteration.cjs` -- route-proof sources and delta fallback.\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/dispatch-iteration.cjs` -- prompt-file reads, temp-dir creation, flag validation and env pass-through.\\n- Traceability sources: `007-source-diversity-quality-class` REQ-001..REQ-005, `001-completed-run-extension` REQ-002, `006-state-log-leaf-fields` REQ-001..REQ-004.\\n\\n## Findings - New\\n\\n### P0 Findings\\n\\nNone.\\n\\n### P1 Findings\\n\\nNone.\\n\\n### P2 Findings\\n\\n1. **Source-quality classification assumes POSIX separators, so Windows-separated paths lose their spec and test classes** -- .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/upsert.cjs:330 -- `classifySourceLocation` strips a leading `./` and splits the path on `/` only, then tests `segments[0] === 'specs'` and `segments.some(segment => TEST_DIR_NAMES.has(segment))`. A path written with backslashes, as a Windows leaf would cite it (`specs\\\\\\\\foo\\\\\\\\spec.md` or `src\\\\\\\\tests\\\\\\\\fixture.ts`), becomes a single segment; `specs\\\\\\\\...` then falls through to `SKILL_DOC` because of `.md`, and a test-directory file falls through to `REPO_CODE`. The graph's source-diversity signal then reads a different class set for the same repository on Windows than on macOS or Linux, and the REQ-002 class table is only pinned for POSIX paths.\\nFinding class: class-of-bug\\nScope proof: the only separator handling in the function is `split('/')` (`:330`); `path.extname` still finds the extension because it accepts both separators, so the function returns a class rather than failing, and no test in the REQ-002 case uses a backslash path.\\nAffected surface hints: [\\\"upsert.cjs classifySourceLocation\\\", \\\"coverage-graph-signals sourceDiversity\\\", \\\"007 REQ-002 class table\\\", \\\"Windows leaf citation form\\\"]\\nCase: a research leaf on Windows cites `specs\\\\\\\\system-deep-loop\\\\\\\\...\\\\\\\\spec.md` and `src\\\\\\\\tests\\\\\\\\fixture.ts`; the upsert stores `skill-doc` and `repo-code` where a macOS run of the same files stores `repo-spec` and `repo-test`, so a question that cites both a spec and a test can score 2.0 on one host and 1.0 on another.\\n\\n## Traceability Checks\\n\\n| Protocol | Status | Evidence |\\n|----------|--------|----------|\\n| `spec_code` | partial | `007` REQ-001..REQ-005, `001` REQ-002 and `006` REQ-001..REQ-004 match the code on POSIX paths and on the extension ledger shape. The iteration 3 REQ-007 admission gap remains the only partial; this iteration adds the Windows path-shape gap above as an advisory. |\\n| `checklist_evidence` | partial | `007` REQ-002's class table is met for the POSIX fixtures it names; AC-008 remains the outstanding partial. |\\n\\n## Integration Evidence\\n\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/upsert.cjs:383-410` reads the state log and delta only when the review pipeline passes `--state-log`/`--delta-dir`, and rejects those flags for research; the namespace it writes is `{ specFolder, loopType, sessionId }`.\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2840-2860` loads every delta file under the packet's `deltas/` directory and hands the flattened rows to one registry build; a malformed delta can only affect derived output inside the packet.\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/verify-iteration.cjs:552-570` prefers the projected route proof and falls back to the delta only when the state row fails it, with the fallback named on stderr.\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/dispatch-iteration.cjs:273-300` reads the prompt file the workflow staged and never interpolates argv values into a shell; the executor command is built from an argv array.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- `dispatch-iteration.cjs:835` creates its review baseline directory under `process.env.TMPDIR || '/tmp'` rather than `os.tmpdir()`; the line predates this range, and on a host without `/tmp` it would fail rather than escape the packet, so it is recorded as a portability observation, not a finding.\\n- The reducer writes registry, dashboard and strategy through `fs.writeFileSync` without an lstat symlink guard; a leaf-writable lineage directory could hold a symlink at one of those names. The write helpers predate the range, and the orchestrator's containment snapshot covers outside-lineage effects, so this is recorded as a residual hardening item rather than a range finding.\\n- A corrupt lineage state log aborts the whole merge rather than degrading that lineage; the merge's own per-lineage reconstruction path documents why that is undesirable, but the throwing readers predate the range.\\n\\n## Confirmed-Clean Surfaces\\n\\n- The upsert never trusts a leaf-supplied `quality_class` for research SOURCE nodes; the derived class overwrites it, and the closed vocabulary is imported from the graph query module.\\n- Finding evidence is derived only from a `file:line` ref, and an edge whose endpoint neither the batch nor the stored graph declares is skipped with a stderr notice instead of failing the batch.\\n- The review and research reducers read all state under the packet's own artifact directory; no flag can redirect the registry or dashboard path.\\n- `convergence.cjs` reads stop facts from the registry and state rows only; no new external input reaches the stop decision.\\n- No range-introduced script in this node invokes a shell or a GNU-only tool; the new code uses Node `fs`/`path` and JSON parsing.\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- A symlink-following write through the reducer outputs as a range finding: the writers predate the range and the effect stays inside or is caught by containment.\\n- A corrupt-lineage merge abort as a range finding: the readers predate the range and fail closed.\\n- The `/tmp` fallback as a range finding: the line predates the range; recorded as a portability observation.\\n- Treating leaf-authored finding paths as an injection boundary: they are already rendered as untrusted prose in every review artifact, and no new evaluator consumes them.\\n\\n## Next Focus\\n\\n- Dimension: security on F-1-007 (pivot and coverage-graph trust boundaries).\\n- Reason: F-1-005 security is clear apart from the Windows advisory; F-1-007 is the last node without security coverage and its pivot state, paths and graph signals are the remaining untrusted-input surface.\\n- Required evidence: `divergent-pivot.ts` persisted-config parsing and path containment, `divergent-pivot.cjs` argv parsing, `frontier-admit.cjs graph` output and `coverage-graph` metadata filtering.\\n- Rotation status: correctness covered on all nodes; security covered on F-1-003 and F-1-005.\\n\\nReview verdict: CONDITIONAL\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/review/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1795115944558,"end":1795115956208}}}}
