{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1795116040874,"sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","part":{"id":"prt_1f54102aa001KK0KlbiC6go1H8","sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","messageID":"msg_1f540f821001tv9gSIPpvPQmOH","type":"step-start","snapshot":"cee2bcdea95f045f76156e6111232dd390d0b4dc"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1795116042775,"sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","part":{"partID":"prt_1f5410908001v5xJlHN1tQSwVW","sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","messageID":"msg_1f540f821001tv9gSIPpvPQmOH","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f90cc2aaf8b2ceb","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/455-expansive-depth-integration && grep -n \"pivot\\|frontier\\|admit\" .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/manual-testing-playbook/convergence-and-recovery/review-pivot-gates-on-terminal-frontier.md | sed -n '1,25p'"},"output":"2:title: \"DRV-081 -- Review pivot waits for a terminal frontier\"\n3:description: \"This scenario validates that under the expansive mode the review divergent pivot refuses to run while a frontier node stays open, seeds one revisit direction per closed sub-target the frontier left uncovered and leaves a default run untouched, for `DRV-081`.\"\n7:# DRV-081 -- Review pivot waits for a terminal frontier\n15:This scenario validates the review divergent pivot gate for `DRV-081`. It focuses on the refusal while a frontier node is open, on the revisit directions the pivot seeds from sub-targets the frontier closed without covering every dimension, and on the default run, which keeps the pivot exactly as before.\n19:The pivot reopens the review from a new angle. If it ran while a sub-target was still open, it would compete with the frontier for the same ground. If it ignored the sub-targets the frontier gave up on, those sub-targets would never get a second look.\n25:Operators run the commands below and confirm that the pivot waits for a terminal frontier, seeds a revisit for each closed sub-target it left uncovered, and leaves the default run alone.\n27:- Objective: Confirm that the review pivot refuses to run while a frontier node is open, that it seeds one `frontier-unanswered` direction per closed sub-target the frontier left uncovered, and that a default run seeds no frontier direction even when its registry carries an open node.\n28:- Real user request: Why did the review skip its divergent pivot, and does it still check the sub-targets it gave up on?\n29:- Prompt: `Check that the deep-review divergent pivot refuses to run while a frontier node is open, seeds one revisit direction for each closed sub-target the frontier left uncovered, and leaves a default run untouched. Return a PASS or FAIL verdict with the reason.`\n30:- Expected execution process: Run the review pivot cases by file stem, run the workflow case that gates the pivot on a terminal frontier, then read the line in the pivot script that labels a seed `frontier-unanswered`.\n31:- Expected signals: The five pivot cases pass. The refusal case throws for an open node. The seed case creates one direction for the exhausted sub-target and none for the answered one. The default-run case keeps no frontier direction. The verb-scope case keeps a seed whose scope path names a mutation verb. The free-text-scope case rejects a seed whose scope asks for a change. The workflow gate case passes, and the pivot script labels the seed `frontier-unanswered`.\n32:- Desired user-visible outcome: The operator can say the pivot waited until the frontier closed, and each sub-target the frontier gave up on got one revisit direction.\n33:- Pass/fail: PASS if the pivot cases, the gate case and the seed label all pass or print; FAIL if the pivot runs while a node is open, an answered sub-target gets a revisit, or a default run seeds a frontier direction.\n46:- Prompt: `Check that the deep-review divergent pivot refuses to run while a frontier node is open, seeds one revisit direction for each closed sub-target the frontier left uncovered, and leaves a default run untouched. Return a PASS or FAIL verdict with the reason.`\n50:1. `cd .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime && npx vitest run divergent-pivot.vitest -t \"review divergent pivot under the expansive depth mode\" --reporter=verbose`\n51:2. `cd .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime && npx vitest run stop-decision-yaml-contract.vitest -t \"gates the divergent pivot on a terminal review frontier\" --reporter=verbose`\n52:3. `rg -n \"frontier-unanswered\" .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/scripts/divergent-review-pivot.ts`\n56:Command 1 prints `Tests  5 passed` (the rest of the file skipped). Its five passing cases are `refuses a pivot while an open frontier node remains`, `seeds one frontier-unanswered direction per closed sub-target the frontier left uncovered`, `keeps a frontier seed whose scope path happens to name a mutation verb`, `still rejects a frontier seed whose free-text scope asks for a change` and `leaves a default run untouched even when the registry still carries an open frontier node`. Command 2 prints `Tests  1 passed` (the rest of the file skipped), and the case `gates the divergent pivot on a terminal review frontier` passes. Command 3 prints one line, which sets the seed source to `frontier-unanswered`.\n61:- The line from command 3, read from the current pivot script.\n66:- **Fail**: The pivot runs while a node is open, an answered sub-target gets a revisit, a default run seeds a frontier direction, or command 3 prints nothing.\n70:1. The pivot runs while a node is open: check the frontier gate in the review workflow, and count the open and active nodes in the registry it reads.\n71:2. An answered sub-target gets a revisit: the seed must take only sub-targets the frontier closed without covering every dimension, so check the status filter in the pivot script.\n72:3. A default run seeds a frontier direction: check that the frontier seed only runs under the expansive mode, so a default registry with a stray node stays untouched.\n89:| `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/scripts/divergent-review-pivot.ts` | The frontier gate, and the `frontier-unanswered` seed for closed sub-targets |\n90:| `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review.yaml` | The `frontier_gate` on the divergent pivot, which allows it only on a terminal frontier |\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2:title: \"DRV-081 -- Review pivot waits for a terminal frontier\"\n3:description: \"This scenario validates that under the expansive mode the review divergent pivot refuses to run while a frontier node stays open, seeds one revisit direction per closed sub-target the frontier left uncovered and leaves a default run untouched, for `DRV-081`.\"\n7:# DRV-081 -- Review pivot waits for a terminal frontier\n15:This scenario validates the review divergent pivot gate for `DRV-081`. It focuses on the refusal while a frontier node is open, on the revisit directions the pivot seeds from sub-targets the frontier closed without covering every dimension, and on the default run, which keeps the pivot exactly as before.\n19:The pivot reopens the review from a new angle. If it ran while a sub-target was still open, it would compete with the frontier for the same ground. If it ignored the sub-targets the frontier gave up on, those sub-targets would never get a second look.\n25:Operators run the commands below and confirm that the pivot waits for a terminal frontier, seeds a revisit for each closed sub-target it left uncovered, and leaves the default run alone.\n27:- Objective: Confirm that the review pivot refuses to run while a frontier node is open, that it seeds one `frontier-unanswered` direction per closed sub-target the frontier left uncovered, and that a default run seeds no frontier direction even when its registry carries an open node.\n28:- Real user request: Why did the review skip its divergent pivot, and does it still check the sub-targets it gave up on?\n29:- Prompt: `Check that the deep-review divergent pivot refuses to run while a frontier node is open, seeds one revisit direction for each closed sub-target the frontier left uncovered, and leaves a default run untouched. Return a PASS or FAIL verdict with the reason.`\n30:- Expected execution process: Run the review pivot cases by file stem, run the workflow case that gates the pivot on a terminal frontier, then read the line in the pivot script that labels a seed `frontier-unanswered`.\n31:- Expected signals: The five pivot cases pass. The refusal case throws for an open node. The seed case creates one direction for the exhausted sub-target and none for the answered one. The default-run case keeps no frontier direction. The verb-scope case keeps a seed whose scope path names a mutation verb. The free-text-scope case rejects a seed whose scope asks for a change. The workflow gate case passes, and the pivot script labels the seed `frontier-unanswered`.\n32:- Desired user-visible outcome: The operator can say the pivot waited until the frontier closed, and each sub-target the frontier gave up on got one revisit direction.\n33:- Pass/fail: PASS if the pivot cases, the gate case and the seed label all pass or print; FAIL if the pivot runs while a node is open, an answered sub-target gets a revisit, or a default run seeds a frontier direction.\n46:- Prompt: `Check that the deep-review divergent pivot refuses to run while a frontier node is open, seeds one revisit direction for each closed sub-target the frontier left uncovered, and leaves a default run untouched. Return a PASS or FAIL verdict with the reason.`\n50:1. `cd .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime && npx vitest run divergent-pivot.vitest -t \"review divergent pivot under the expansive depth mode\" --reporter=verbose`\n51:2. `cd .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime && npx vitest run stop-decision-yaml-contract.vitest -t \"gates the divergent pivot on a terminal review frontier\" --reporter=verbose`\n52:3. `rg -n \"frontier-unanswered\" .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/scripts/divergent-review-pivot.ts`\n56:Command 1 prints `Tests  5 passed` (the rest of the file skipped). Its five passing cases are `refuses a pivot while an open frontier node remains`, `seeds one frontier-unanswered direction per closed sub-target the frontier left uncovered`, `keeps a frontier seed whose scope path happens to name a mutation verb`, `still rejects a frontier seed whose free-text scope asks for a change` and `leaves a default run untouched even when the registry still carries an open frontier node`. Command 2 prints `Tests  1 passed` (the rest of the file skipped), and the case `gates the divergent pivot on a terminal review frontier` passes. Command 3 prints one line, which sets the seed source to `frontier-unanswered`.\n61:- The line from command 3, read from the current pivot script.\n66:- **Fail**: The pivot runs while a node is open, an answered sub-target gets a revisit, a default run seeds a frontier direction, or command 3 prints nothing.\n70:1. The pivot runs while a node is open: check the frontier gate in the review workflow, and count the open and active nodes in the registry it reads.\n71:2. An answered sub-target gets a revisit: the seed must take only sub-targets the frontier closed without covering every dimension, so check the status filter in the pivot script.\n72:3. A default run seeds a frontier direction: check that the frontier seed only runs under the expansive mode, so a default registry with a stray node stays untouched.\n89:| `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/scripts/divergent-review-pivot.ts` | The frontier gate, and the `frontier-unanswered` seed for closed sub-targets |\n90:| `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review.yaml` | The `frontier_gate` on the divergent pivot, which allows it only on a terminal frontier |\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/455-expansive-depth-integration && grep -n \\\"pivot\\\\|frontier\\\\|admit\\\" .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/manual-testing-playbook/convergence-and-recovery/review-pivot-gates-on-terminal-frontier.md | sed -n '1,25p'\"}"},"time":{"start":1795116042504,"end":1795116042775}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1795116042783,"sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","part":{"partID":"prt_1f54107a5001zQWVRPmmeAGSxN","sessionID":"ses_e0acc529fffetdTx9mE3m9n6Pl","messageID":"msg_1f540f821001tv9gSIPpvPQmOH","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b0f5559cf95accd4","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/455-expansive-depth-integration && grep -n \"maxRootChildren\" -A 3 .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/frontier-admit.cjs | head -20; echo \"===review catalog claims===\"; sed -n '14,40p' .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/feature-catalog/loop-lifecycle/expansive-depth-mode.md"},"output":"400: * @returns {{maxDepth: number, maxChildrenPerNode: number, maxRootChildren: number, maxFrontierNodes: number}} The caps.\n401- * @throws {Error} When the config exists but cannot be read or parsed.\n402- */\n403-function readCaps(artifactDir, target = 'research') {\n--\n424:    maxRootChildren: Math.max(maxChildrenPerNode, ROOT_CHILDREN_FLOOR[target]),\n425-    maxFrontierNodes: runCap,\n426-  };\n427-}\n--\n533:  return parentId === 'root' && Number.isFinite(caps.maxRootChildren) ? caps.maxRootChildren : caps.maxChildrenPerNode;\n534-}\n535-\n536-// A review target is a file list, and only those paths can hold a finding. The\n===review catalog claims===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n`--depth-mode` takes `default` or `expansive`. A review without the flag is unchanged. `expansive` seeds depth-one sub-target nodes from the review target before iteration 1, admits leaf `frontier_proposal` rows under the research caps with two differences, a root-children cap of at least 8 against research's 6 and a relevance check that prunes a proposal scoped outside a file-list review target, keeps node status in `review/frontier-nodes.jsonl`, and holds open nodes as `searchDebt`. This page describes the shipped code and its tests.\n\n---\n\n## 2. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Seeding And Admission\n\nThe expansive run seeds depth-one sub-target nodes from the review target before iteration 1. Leaf `frontier_proposal` rows are admitted under relevance, dedupe, depth, fan-out, budget and cycle checks, in that order. On a commit-range target, the seed prunes a merge commit with no diff of its own as `merge-no-own-diff` and a generated index rebuild as `generated-artifact`.\n\nA defaulted review budget is capped at 24, so the depth-1 band gets at most 12 iterations, which funds three nodes at four dimensions. With four or more depth-1 nodes, raise `--max-iterations` or use fan-out.\n\n### Dimension Coverage\n\nThe active node's next uncovered dimension is dispatched, apart from the last iteration under a cap of 2 or more while a core traceability protocol is unfinished and `traceability` is among the run's dimensions, which reviews `traceability`. A run whose `reviewDimensions` list leaves traceability out is not steered to it, so its last iteration reviews the configured set. A node needs one iteration per dimension in its universe, the four canonical dimensions plus any custom name. Only an attempt that produced evidence counts as a frontier visit, so an iteration record with status `error`, `failed`, `failure` or `timeout` covers no dimension, spends no band share and does not count for the focus tie-break. Each depth band starts from its `budgetSplit` share of `--max-iterations` (0.5, 0.35 and 0.15), plus any share an earlier band left unspent once that band holds no open node, so set `--max-iterations` to at least `2 * ceil(nodes / lineages) * dimensions`, which gives the depth-1 band `ceil(nodes / lineages) * dimensions` iterations. A store row that does not parse is skipped, and the reducer reports it on stderr and in the frontier projection's `warnings` with its file and line. The run-wide `maxFrontierNodes` cap defaults to half of `maxIterations`, and every seed that is not pruned counts toward it. `--frontier-max-depth=N` and `--frontier-max-nodes=N` set `maxDepth` and `maxFrontierNodes` when the config is created; both take a positive integer, need `--depth-mode=expansive` on a single-executor run, and are refused on a fan-out config. When `--max-iterations` is left at its default, the run sizes its budget from the seed: `min(24, max(7, 2 × D × (S + maxDepth − 1)))` iterations for S seeded depth-1 nodes and D the four canonical dimensions plus each custom name the config's `reviewDimensions` list adds, each counted once, and the seed step prints the derived budget. Since D is never below four, the ceiling of 24 binds from the first seed, so one run cannot fund every node at every depth of a wide review; split a wide review with fan-out, where each lineage with no matrix `iterations` value sizes its own budget from its share of the seed, with D fixed at the canonical four because a lineage reviews only those. A lineage dealt no seed node, or a run whose seeds were all pruned, runs at its loop default instead: 12 iterations for a native lineage and 7 for a CLI one or a single run. The node cap stays half the budget, which leaves room for at least two nodes past depth 1. An explicit `--max-iterations`, marker, setup answer or fan-out matrix `iterations` value always wins, even below the derived value, and the seed warns when it leaves no room past depth 1. At about seven to eight minutes an iteration, a 24-iteration review can reach `maxDurationMinutes` (120) before its iteration cap.\n\n### Stop Behavior\n\nA review stop candidate becomes another iteration while nodes stay open. The `frontierOpen` veto applies to the review stop candidates, including `all_dimensions_clean` and the composite stop, after the minIterations floor. Once every node is closed and at least one was answered, a run that forms no other stop candidate gets `frontier_drained`, which the legal-stop gates still decide.\n\n### Dashboard\n\nAn expansive run adds a `## 10B. FRONTIER` section to its review dashboard, and a default run renders no such section. The section reports the frontier size by status, the pruned count, the revisits that covered no new dimension, each budget band's spent and allotted iterations, and the active node with its next dimension.\n\n### Pivot\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"400: * @returns {{maxDepth: number, maxChildrenPerNode: number, maxRootChildren: number, maxFrontierNodes: number}} The caps.\n401- * @throws {Error} When the config exists but cannot be read or parsed.\n402- */\n403-function readCaps(artifactDir, target = 'research') {\n--\n424:    maxRootChildren: Math.max(maxChildrenPerNode, ROOT_CHILDREN_FLOOR[target]),\n425-    maxFrontierNodes: runCap,\n426-  };\n427-}\n--\n533:  return parentId === 'root' && Number.isFinite(caps.maxRootChildren) ? caps.maxRootChildren : caps.maxChildrenPerNode;\n534-}\n535-\n536-// A review target is a file list, and only those paths can hold a finding. The\n===review catalog claims===\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n`--depth-mode` takes `default` or `expansive`. A review without the flag is unchanged. `expansive` seeds depth-one sub-target nodes from the review target before iteration 1, admits leaf `frontier_proposal` rows under the research caps with two differences, a root-children cap of at least 8 against research's 6 and a relevance check that prunes a proposal scoped outside a file-list review target, keeps node status in `review/frontier-nodes.jsonl`, and holds open nodes as `searchDebt`. This page describes the shipped code and its tests.\n\n---\n\n## 2. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Seeding And Admission\n\nThe expansive run seeds depth-one sub-target nodes from the review target before iteration 1. Leaf `frontier_proposal` rows are admitted under relevance, dedupe, depth, fan-out, budget and cycle checks, in that order. On a commit-range target, the seed prunes a merge commit with no diff of its own as `merge-no-own-diff` and a generated index rebuild as `generated-artifact`.\n\nA defaulted review budget is capped at 24, so the depth-1 band gets at most 12 iterations, which funds three nodes at four dimensions. With four or more depth-1 nodes, raise `--max-iterations` or use fan-out.\n\n### Dimension Coverage\n\nThe active node's next uncovered dimension is dispatched, apart from the last iteration under a cap of 2 or more while a core traceability protocol is unfinished and `traceability` is among the run's dimensions, which reviews `traceability`. A run whose `reviewDimensions` list leaves traceability out is not steered to it, so its last iteration reviews the configured set. A node needs one iteration per dimension in its universe, the four canonical dimensions plus any custom name. Only an attempt that produced evidence counts as a frontier visit, so an iteration record with status `error`, `failed`, `failure` or `timeout` covers no dimension, spends no band share and does not count for the focus tie-break. Each depth band starts from its `budgetSplit` share of `--max-iterations` (0.5, 0.35 and 0.15), plus any share an earlier band left unspent once that band holds no open node, so set `--max-iterations` to at least `2 * ceil(nodes / lineages) * dimensions`, which gives the depth-1 band `ceil(nodes / lineages) * dimensions` iterations. A store row that does not parse is skipped, and the reducer reports it on stderr and in the frontier projection's `warnings` with its file and line. The run-wide `maxFrontierNodes` cap defaults to half of `maxIterations`, and every seed that is not pruned counts toward it. `--frontier-max-depth=N` and `--frontier-max-nodes=N` set `maxDepth` and `maxFrontierNodes` when the config is created; both take a positive integer, need `--depth-mode=expansive` on a single-executor run, and are refused on a fan-out config. When `--max-iterations` is left at its default, the run sizes its budget from the seed: `min(24, max(7, 2 × D × (S + maxDepth − 1)))` iterations for S seeded depth-1 nodes and D the four canonical dimensions plus each custom name the config's `reviewDimensions` list adds, each counted once, and the seed step prints the derived budget. Since D is never below four, the ceiling of 24 binds from the first seed, so one run cannot fund every node at every depth of a wide review; split a wide review with fan-out, where each lineage with no matrix `iterations` value sizes its own budget from its share of the seed, with D fixed at the canonical four because a lineage reviews only those. A lineage dealt no seed node, or a run whose seeds were all pruned, runs at its loop default instead: 12 iterations for a native lineage and 7 for a CLI one or a single run. The node cap stays half the budget, which leaves room for at least two nodes past depth 1. An explicit `--max-iterations`, marker, setup answer or fan-out matrix `iterations` value always wins, even below the derived value, and the seed warns when it leaves no room past depth 1. At about seven to eight minutes an iteration, a 24-iteration review can reach `maxDurationMinutes` (120) before its iteration cap.\n\n### Stop Behavior\n\nA review stop candidate becomes another iteration while nodes stay open. The `frontierOpen` veto applies to the review stop candidates, including `all_dimensions_clean` and the composite stop, after the minIterations floor. Once every node is closed and at least one was answered, a run that forms no other stop candidate gets `frontier_drained`, which the legal-stop gates still decide.\n\n### Dashboard\n\nAn expansive run adds a `## 10B. FRONTIER` section to its review dashboard, and a default run renders no such section. The section reports the frontier size by status, the pruned count, the revisits that covered no new dimension, each budget band's spent and allotted iterations, and the active node with its next dimension.\n\n### Pivot\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/455-expansive-depth-integration && grep -n \\\"maxRootChildren\\\" -A 3 .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/frontier-admit.cjs | head -20; echo \\\"===review catalog claims===\\\"; sed -n '14,40p' .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/feature-catalog/loop-lifecycle/expansive-depth-mode.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1795116042149,"end":1795116042783}}}}
